misc: FPC1264 power/reset/IRQ control (secure-world SPI)

The sensor's SPI bus belongs to the secure world.  The OEM fpcliu trusted
application drives it over QSEE's SPI service: the trustlet image contains
qsee_spi_open/full_duplex/write and fpc_pal_spi_* (strings in
fpcliu.mdt/b* from the stock firmware), the vendor Android DT keeps
spi@a88000 disabled, and the stock fpc1552.ko contains no SPI code at all
-- it only powers the sensor, pulses reset and reports the finger IRQ,
with a comment that the TEE side must soft-reset the sensor because the AP
cannot control chip select.

Do the same here: a platform driver on /dev/fpc1020 that powers the
sensor (PM8350C LDO9, 100 mA), reproduces the vendor reset pulse
(release, 100 us, assert, 5 ms, release, 5 ms), reports the GPIO40 rising
edge through poll() and enables the interrupt as a wakeup source.  It
performs no SPI transfers and does not touch the QUP serial engine, so the
normal world cannot race the trustlet's SPI master.

This replaces the earlier port of the community SPI-passthrough driver
(their own userspace never used its SPI path -- it only opens /dev/fpc1020
to power the sensor and polls for the interrupt -- while enabling spi10
contended with the secure world for QUP1 SE3, the mechanism behind the
"GENI/pinctrl corruption" that community bring-up left unexplained).

Provenance: electrical sequence and pinctrl configuration reproduced from
the stock 5.10 tree (drivers/input/fingerprint/fpc_ta/fpc1020_tee.c) and
the vendor runtime DT (fingerprint_fpc node).

The driver also exposes a read-only power_state attribute (the community
driver had one on its SPI device); the OEM runtime verifies the sensor
powered down after an operation through it.

